-5-101†doi.org】\nL36: # Genome-scale reconstruction and in silico analysis of the Ralstonia eutropha H16 for polyhydroxyalkanoate synthesis, lithoautotrophic growth, and 2-methyl citric acid production\nL37: \nL38: 【13†Jong Myoung Park†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL39: ### Jong Myoung Park\nL40: \nL41: ^{1}Metabolic and Biomolecular Engineering National Research Laboratory, Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (BK21 program), KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL42: \nL43: ^{2}BioProcess Engineering Research Center, and Center for Systems and Synthetic Biotechnology, Institute for the BioCentury, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL44: \nL45: Find articles by 【13†Jong Myoung Park†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL46: ^{1,}^{2}, 【14†Tae Yong Kim†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL47: ### Tae Yong Kim\nL48: \nL49: ^{1}Metabolic and Biomolecular Engineering National Research Laboratory, Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (BK21 program), KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL50: \nL51: ^{3}Bioinformatics Research Center, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL52: \nL53: Find articles by 【14†Tae Yong Kim†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL54: \nL55: ^{1,}^{3}, 【15†Sang Yup Lee†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL56: ### Sang Yup Lee\nL57: \nL58: ^{1}Metabolic and Biomolecular Engineering National Research Laboratory, Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (BK21 program), KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL59: \nL60: ^{2}BioProcess Engineering Research Center, and Center for Systems and Synthetic Biotechnology, Institute for the BioCentury, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL61: ^{3}Bioinformatics Research Center, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL62: \nL63: Find articles by 【15†Sang Yup Lee†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL64: \nL65: ^{1,}^{2,}^{3,}^{✉}\nL66: \nL67:   * [Button: Author information]\nL68:   * [Button: Article notes]\nL69:   * [Button: Copyright and License information]\nL70: \nL71: ^{1}Metabolic and Biomolecular Engineering National Research Laboratory, Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (BK21 program), KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL72: ^{2}BioProcess Engineering Research Center, and Center for Systems and Synthetic Biotechnology, Institute for the BioCentury, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL73: \nL74: ^{3}Bioinformatics Research Center, KAIST, 291 Daehak-ro, Yuseong-gu, Daejeon 305-701, Republic of Korea\nL75: \nL76: ^{✉}\nL77: \nL78: Corresponding author.\nL79: \nL80: Received 2011 Mar 27; Accepted 2011 Jun 28; Collection date 2011.\nL81: \nL82: Copyright ©2011 Park et al; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.\nL83: This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (【16†http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0†creativecommons.org】), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\nL84: \nL85: 【17†PMC Copyright notice】\nL86: \nL87: PMCID: PMC3154180  PMID: 【18†21711532†pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL88: ## Abstract\nL89: ### Background\nL90: Ralstonia eutropha H16, found in both soil and water, is a Gram-negative lithoautotrophic bacterium that can utillize CO_{2 }and H_{2 }as its sources of carbon and energy in the absence of organic substrates. R. eutropha H16 can reach high cell densities either under lithoautotrophic or heterotrophic conditions, which makes it suitable for a number of biotechnological applications.\nL91: It is the best known and most promising producer of polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHAs) from various carbon substrates and is an environmentally important bacterium that can degrade aromatic compounds. In order to make R. eutropha H16 a more efficient and robust biofactory, system-wide metabolic engineering to improve its metabolic performance is essential. Thus, it is necessary to analyze its metabolic characteristics systematically and optimize the entire metabolic network at systems level.\nL92: ### Results\nL93: We present the lithoautotrophic genome-scale metabolic model of R. eutropha H16 based on the annotated genome with biochemical and physiological information. The stoichiometic model, RehMBEL1391, is composed of 1391 reactions including 229 transport reactions and 1171 metabolites. Constraints-based flux analyses were performed to refine and validate the genome-scale metabolic model under environmental and genetic perturbations. First, the lithoautotrophic growth characteristics of R.\nL94: eutropha H16 were investigated under varying feeding ratios of gas mixture. Second, the genome-scale metabolic model was used to design the strategies for the production of poly[R-(-)-3hydroxybutyrate] (PHB) under different pH values and carbon/nitrogen source uptake ratios. It was also used to analyze the metabolic characteristics of R. eutropha when the phosphofructokinase gene was expressed.\nL95: Finally, in silico gene knockout simulations were performed to identify targets for metabolic engineering essential for the production of 2-methylcitric acid in R. eutropha H16.\nL96: ### Conclusion\nL97: \nL98: The genome-scale metabolic model, RehMBEL1391, successfully represented metabolic characteristics of R. eutropha H16 at systems level. The reconstructed genome-scale metabolic model can be employed as an useful tool for understanding its metabolic capabilities, predicting its physiological consequences in response to various environmental and genetic changes, and developing strategies for systems metabolic engineering to improve its metabolic performance.\nL99: ## Background\nL100: Ralstonia eutropha H16, also known as Wautersia eutropha H16 or Cupriavidus necator H16, is a nonpathogenic Gram-negative bacterium, found in both soil and water, belonging to the Betaproteobacteria. It is a lithoautotrophic bacterium that utilizes CO_{2 }and H_{2 }as sources of carbon and energy in the absence of organic substrates, and is an environmentally important bacterium that can degrade various aromatic compounds [【19†1】].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChecking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20511417/)\nciteturn42view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20511417/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\nL0: Checking your browser before accessing pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ...\nL1: \nL2: Click 【0†here】 if you are not automatically redirected after 5 seconds.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDevelopment of chemically defined medium for Mannheimia succiniciproducens based on its genome sequence - PubMed (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18379780/)\nciteturn42view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18379780/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 400\nL0: 【0†Skip to main page content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Log in†account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】 Show account info\nL3: \nL4: [Input]\nL5: \nL6: Search: [Input: ]Search\nL7: \nL8: 【2†Advanced】\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Save ][Button: Email ]\nL11: \nL12: [Button: Send to]\nL13: \nL14:   * 【3†Clipboard】\nL15:   * 【4†My Bibliography†account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL16:   * 【5†Collections†account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov】\nL17:   * 【3†Citation manager】\nL18: \nL19: Display options\nL20: \nL21: Display options\nL22: \nL23: Format [Select]\nL24: \nL25: [Input]\nL26: \nL27: ## Save citation to file\nL28: \nL29: [Input]\nL30: \nL31: Format: [Select]\nL32: \nL33: [Button: Create file ] [Button: Cancel ]\nL34: ## Email citation\nL35: \nL36: Email address has not been verified. Go to 【6†My N